# Q1: How would a human mime this in empty space? (One sentence)
A human would mime grabbing a handle at their side, lifting the heavy can with effort, tilting their wrist forward to pour, and then sweeping their arm from side to side to water an area.
# Q2: Candidates Comparison. Rank at least 2 (dir, gripper_orientation) pairs by EE geometry (e.g., P1: up+horizontal vs P2: front+vertical). State the winner and why—do NOT pick dir from gesture names alone.
P1: dir="down", gripper_orientation="vertical". The EE points down, with jaws aligned vertically, which perfectly mimics gripping the top handle of a standard watering can. P2: dir="front", gripper_orientation="horizontal". This would imply holding a handle on the back of the can, which is less common and iconic. Winner: P1 is the clearest representation of holding a watering can.
# Q3: Structure Comparison. Compare 2+ primitive sequences (e.g., M1: pose > path arc vs M2: pose > movement rep). State the winner and why.
M1: Pose (grab) > Path (lift) > Movement (tilt) > Path (sweep). This tells a clear, sequential story: get the can, lift it, pour, and water. M2: Pose (grab and tilt) > Path (combined lift and sweep). This merges actions, making the individual parts of the mime harder to distinguish. Winner: M1 is superior for its narrative clarity.
# Q4: Context Reinforcement. Compare 2+ small surrounding actions that make the mime interpretation stronger and pick the best one.
A1: Use a slow speed on the initial "lift" path to imply the watering can is heavy and full of water. A2: Add a brief hold after the wrist "tilt" to establish that the pouring has begun before the sweeping motion starts. Winner: Both are effective and will be used; the slow lift (A1) followed by the tilt and hold (A2) creates the strongest mime.
